  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 154 PM EDT Mon Aug 3 2026\n\nEarly afternoon water vapor imagery and RAP analysis show midlevel \nridging extending from the central Rockies NEwd into the upper Great \nLakes. Meanwhile an anomalous low is centered over northern \nManitoba. A couple of notable shortwave troughs are orbiting this \nlow, one of which is generating an area of rainfall over northern MN \ninto southwestern ON, and another is in eastern Montana. The ridging \nwill continue to promote dry conditions over the local area into \ntonight, although high clouds will increase. \n\nBoth of the aforementioned shortwave troughs will track NEwd, with \ntheir associated forcing passing north of the forecast area. Broad \ntroughing/weak height falls will expand across the UP late tonight \ninto tomorrow, with an associated frontal boundary passing through \nfrom west to east. Area of showers and a few embedded storms \nassociated with midlevel theta-e advection will likely reach the far \nwestern UP around daybreak Tuesday, with coverage waning with \neastward extend. MUCAPE generally stays below 500 J/kg, so any \nstorms are not expected to be strong/severe. Best chance for surface-\nbased destabilization will occur over the eastern half during the \nlate afternoon into the evening Tuesday, but SBCAPE is forecast to \nremain below 750 J/kg, so severe weather is not expected. Some \nquestion whether surface based instability can even develop given \nmodel soundings depicting a warm nose around 800-850 mb, so coverage \nof storms is expected to remain isolated at best. Highs range from \nthe low 70s west, where FROPA happens earliest, to low 80s east.\n\nWeak ridging builds in the wake of the front for Wednesday, setting \nup a dry and seasonable day with highs mainly in the 70s. Another \nshortwave trough rotates through Thursday, but with a lack of any \nnotable moisture, another dry day is expected. Temperatures are \nexpected to be on a general warming trend late week into the weekend \nas low-level southwesterly flow develops. Thunderstorm potential \nenters the picture again by the weekend as strong quasi-zonal flow \ndevelops between Hudson Bay low and Four Corners high. Unclear \nwhether the reservoir of greater instability will get this far north.",
